# 12. Security Audit and Health Dashboard

Audit AuditScreen.kt and the logic that calculates security metrics.

Verify that every metric is derived from actual vault data and documented rules rather than hardcoded values or placeholder results.

## 12.1 Overall security score

Test:

- Empty vault.
- Vault with one entry.
- Vault containing only strong generated passwords.
- Weak passwords.
- Reused passwords.
- Credentials older than 90 days.
- Accounts without TOTP.
- Missing or incomplete metadata.
- Large vaults.
- Changes after editing or deleting records.

Verify that the score is deterministic for the same data and rules.

Inspect the weighting model and grade boundaries for A+, A, B, C, and F.

Document how each metric affects the overall score. Avoid presenting an arbitrary score as an objectively validated security measurement.

Do not label an account as lacking 2FA when the application has insufficient information to determine whether that external account supports it. Represent unknown or unavailable information accurately.

## 12.2 Weak-password detection

Verify that the dashboard uses the intended strength-analysis method and correctly classifies each test case.

Check that the audit can evaluate credentials without exposing them outside the trusted application process.

## 12.3 Reused-password detection

Test:

- Exact duplicates.
- Different usernames with the same password.
- Different website URLs with the same password.
- Case differences where relevant.
- Empty or missing password values.
- Different encrypted records containing identical plaintext passwords.

Verify that comparison occurs on appropriately handled plaintext within the authorized unlocked context, or uses a justified alternative design.

Do not compare ciphertexts as though they reliably identify duplicate passwords when encryption uses randomized IVs.

Avoid storing plaintext password indexes or reusable plaintext hashes that would unnecessarily increase exposure.

## 12.4 Stale credentials

Verify the exact definition of credential age.

Test dates on either side of the 90-day threshold, missing timestamps, future timestamps, timezone boundaries, and clock changes.

Ensure that creation time and last-modified time are not confused with last-password-change time.

If the application does not know when a password was last changed, do not silently treat the entry creation date as definitive proof.

## 12.5 Missing 2FA

Verify the rule for determining whether an account has TOTP configured.

Distinguish between a stored TOTP secret and verified two-factor authentication on the external service. The application cannot automatically know that an external account has enabled 2FA merely because a secret exists locally.

## 12.6 Tap-to-fix vulnerability cards

Verify that every vulnerability card opens the correct entry and the relevant editor field or workflow.

Test:

- Correct entry identification.
- Correct issue identification.
- Navigation back to the dashboard.
- Saving changes.
- Canceling changes.
- Deleting the affected entry.
- Updating the dashboard after a fix.
- Missing or deleted target records.
- Multiple issues affecting the same entry.

A fix must genuinely resolve the underlying metric rather than merely dismissing the card.

