# 13. Auto-Lock, Clipboard, and Android Security Controls

These features protect secrets outside the cryptographic engine and must be tested independently.

## 13.1 Configurable auto-lock

Verify all supported intervals:

- 30 seconds.
- 1 minute.
- 5 minutes.
- 15 minutes.

Inspect the implementation's definition of inactivity and determine whether it matches the intended security policy.

Test:

- No user interaction.
- Normal user interaction.
- Backgrounding.
- Returning to the app.
- Screen lock.
- Process recreation.
- Device sleep.
- Multiple simultaneous timeout events.
- Changing the timeout in settings.
- Locking during a pending operation.

Use an appropriate monotonic clock for elapsed-time measurements so wall-clock adjustments do not unexpectedly extend or shorten security timers.

Ensure the chosen policy behaves consistently across Activity lifecycle events and application navigation.

Do not rely exclusively on a screen-specific timer if secrets can remain accessible elsewhere in the app.

## 13.2 Secure clipboard auto-clearing

Verify that clipboard clearing occurs after the configured or documented expiration interval.

Test:

- Copying passwords.
- Copying usernames.
- Copying TOTP codes.
- Copying recovery material, if permitted.
- Repeated copy operations.
- Multiple timers.
- Manual locking.
- App backgrounding.
- App termination.
- Clipboard contents changed by another application.
- Supported Android versions and clipboard APIs.

Do not erase unrelated clipboard contents if another application or the user has replaced the copied secret.

Where possible, associate cleanup with the copied value or an appropriate unique operation token, and clear only when the clipboard still contains the value VaultZero placed there.

Do not promise that the application can remove all copies of clipboard data from every Android version, keyboard, clipboard manager, or receiving application.

Verify appropriate handling of Android clipboard notifications and sensitive-clipboard flags where supported.

## 13.3 Screen capture and recent-apps protection

Determine whether sensitive screens should use Android's secure-window mechanisms.

Where required by the security model, verify protection against screenshots, screen recording, and sensitive recent-apps previews.

Test that the protection is applied to all relevant screens and is not accidentally removed during navigation.

Do not claim that screenshots are universally prevented across all devices or external capture methods.

